Look up the handler of the root method for exceptions thrown by inlined code.

A null pointer or an array bound check in compiled code jumps to a shared
thrower, without a handler lookup, when the method has no handler for the
exception. The check took the bci of the compiler that was running, which is
the bci in the inlined method while a call is inlined. The inlined method has
no handlers; the exception goes to the handler of the root method that covers
the call, and the check has to ask about that bci. With the bci inside the
inlined method it often found no handler: an inlined method that threw such an
exception in a try block, for example `int get(int[] a, int i) { return a[i]; }`,
let the exception pass the handler of its caller.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/compiler/CodeGenerator.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/compiler/CodeGenerator.cpp
@@ -688,9 +688,11 @@
   GUARANTEE(rte == ThrowExceptionStub::rte_null_pointer ||
             rte == ThrowExceptionStub::rte_array_index_out_of_bounds,"sanity");
 
+  // Inlined methods have no handlers of their own. An exception they throw
+  // goes to the handler of the root method that covers the inlined call.
   const int handler_bci = m->exception_handler_bci_for(
     ThrowExceptionStub::exception_class(rte),
-    Compiler::current()->bci() JVM_NO_CHECK);
+    Compiler::root()->bci() JVM_NO_CHECK);
   return handler_bci == -1;
 }
 
